      ↓
Azure pulls image
      ↓
Azure creates another Docker Container
      ↓
FastAPI running on public URL

This is the complete and accurate picture of your deployment.

The single most important sentence is:

Azure Container Instance does not replace Docker Containers. It is a service whose job is to create and run Docker Containers from images stored in Azure Container Registry.

That’s exactly what happened when you selected agent-test:latest from the dropdown and clicked Create.









tExcellent. This is the right question.

Most people learn deployment as a list of commands. What actually helps is understanding:

“What problem is this step solving?”

Let’s go through your exact flow.

⸻

Step 1: Build the Image

Command:

docker build -t agent-test:latest .

What is happening?

Docker reads your Dockerfile and creates an image containing:

Python
FastAPI
LangChain
Azure SDKs
Your Code
requirements.txt packages

Result:

agent-test:latest


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Imagine you give Azure only your source code:

main.py
requirements.txt

Azure would ask:

Which Python version?
Which libraries?
How should I start the app?

Azure doesn’t know.

The image answers all those questions.

The image says:

Here is EVERYTHING needed to run my application.


⸻

Significance

The image makes your application portable.

Without image:

Works on my laptop
Might fail on Azure

With image:

Same environment everywhere

Think:

Source Code = Cake Recipe

Docker Image = Ready-to-bake Cake Mix


⸻

Step 2: Run Locally

Command:

docker run -p 6034:6034 agent-test:latest


⸻

What is happening?

Docker creates a container from the image and starts it.

Image
   ↓
Container
   ↓
FastAPI Running


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Because deployment debugging is painful.

Suppose you immediately push to Azure.

Then you discover:

Import Error
Missing package
Wrong port
Startup crash

Now you must:

Fix
Build
Push
Deploy
Wait

every time.

Instead, verify locally first.

⸻

Significance

You are validating:

Can the image actually run?

before involving Azure.

Think:

Test the car engine
before shipping the car overseas.


⸻

Step 3: Open Swagger

URL:

localhost:6034/docs


⸻

What is happening?

FastAPI generated documentation appears.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

Because merely starting isn’t enough.

The application could:

Start Successfully
But APIs may fail

Swagger proves:

Application Started
Routes Loaded
API Reachable


⸻

Significance

This validates business functionality, not just startup.

⸻

Step 4: Azure Login

Command:

az login --use-device-code


⸻

What is happening?

Your CLI gets permission to interact with Azure.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

Without login:

CLI doesn't know:
Who you are
Which company account
Which resources

Azure must authenticate you.

⸻

Significance

This establishes identity.

Think:

Azure Portal Login
=
CLI Login


⸻

Step 5: Select Subscription

You selected:

EA-AZ-DEV-AgenticAI_H&I


⸻

What is happening?

Azure chooses which account’s resources you’re working with.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

A company can have:

Development Subscription
Testing Subscription
Production Subscription

Azure must know:

Where should resources be created?


⸻

Significance

Prevents accidentally deploying into the wrong environment.

⸻

Step 6: Login to ACR

Command:

az acr login --name hexawarejarvisacr


⸻

What is happening?

Azure grants permission to push images into the registry.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

Imagine anyone could upload images.

Someone could upload:

Malicious Code
Virus
Wrong Application

Azure protects the registry.

⸻

Significance

Authentication for image storage.

Think:

GitHub Login
for pushing code

ACR Login
for pushing images


⸻

Step 7: Tag Image

Command:

docker tag agent-test:latest \
hexawarejarvisacr.azurecr.io/agent-test:latest


⸻

What is happening?

You are attaching the registry address to the image.

Before:

agent-test:latest

After:

hexawarejarvisacr.azurecr.io/agent-test:latest


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Docker needs to know:

Which registry should receive this image?

Without tagging:

Docker only knows local image.


⸻

Significance

This is essentially the destination address.

Think:

Letter
+
Recipient Address


⸻

Step 8: Push Image

Command:

docker push


⸻

What is happening?

The image is uploaded from your laptop to ACR.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

Your laptop is not always available.

Azure cannot depend on:

Musaddik's laptop

to start applications.

Azure needs its own copy.

⸻

Significance

Creates a centrally stored deployable artifact.

Think:

Git Push
for source code

Docker Push
for application package


⸻

Step 9: Create Azure Container Instance

Portal:

Create Container Instance


⸻

What is happening?

You tell Azure:

Run this image.


⸻

Why are we doing this?

An image is only a file.

It does nothing until executed.

⸻

Significance

Transforms:

Stored Application

into

Running Application


⸻

Step 10: Select Image from Registry

You selected:

agent-test:latest


⸻

What is happening?

Azure learns which image to run.

⸻

Why are we doing this?

ACR may contain:

claims-agent
underwriter-agent
fraud-agent
document-agent

Azure needs the correct one.

⸻

Significance

Choosing the application version.

⸻

Step 11: Azure Pulls the Image

Behind the scenes:

ACR
   ↓
Download Image


⸻

Why are we doing this?

The image lives in storage.

The runtime machine needs a copy.

⸻

Significance

Moving the package from storage to execution environment.

⸻

Step 12: Azure Creates a Container

Behind the scenes:

Image
   ↓
Container


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Images cannot execute.

Containers execute.

⸻

Significance

This is the moment your code actually starts running.

Think:

Class Blueprint
      ↓
Construct Actual Building


⸻

Step 13: FastAPI Starts

Inside container:

uvicorn main:app


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Your API server must start listening for requests.

⸻

Significance

Without Uvicorn:

Container exists
Application not accessible


⸻

Step 14: Public Endpoint Generated

Example:

https://xxxxx.azurecontainer.io


⸻

Why are we doing this?

Users need a way to reach the application.

⸻

Significance

This converts:

Private Container

into

Accessible Service


⸻

The Entire Flow in One Sentence

Every step exists to solve a specific problem:

Code
 ↓   (Package everything)
Image
 ↓   (Verify it works)
Local Container
 ↓   (Store centrally)
ACR
 ↓   (Run it in cloud)
ACI
 ↓   (Execute image)
Container
 ↓   (Serve requests)
FastAPI API

If you understand what problem each step solves, Azure deployment stops looking like a list of random commands and starts looking like a logical pipeline.
